    questionText: "Explain why each of the following is important in safe food preparation and cooking: Food storage Personal hygiene Reheating procedures",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"questionTotal"}, totalMarks: 6,
    rows: [
    { id: "heading-1", kind: "point", verbatim: "Food storage: store raw and cooked foods in different areas of the fridge to prevent microbial cross contamination; store perishable food below 5°C to help slow down bacterial growth; store frozen food at -18°C; store foods in vermin proof containers; store dry foods in a cool, dry, well ventilated cupboard; remove vegetables and fruit from plastic wrapping to prevent the growth of moulds; when preparing perishable foods, keep out of the fridge for the shortest time to reduce the risk of food poisoning; follow storage instructions on food labels; check ‘use by date’/‘best before date’; cover foods when storing in fridge to prevent food drying out/cross contamination/odour transfer; etc.", marks: null, contextNote: "The scheme prints 6 marks for the complete question, with no per-answer allocation or partial-credit ladder. Check the complete response; no separate answer marks have been inferred. Explain this required heading. Original2018 source wording and temperatures retained." },
    { id: "heading-2", kind: "point", verbatim: "Personal hygiene: wash hands before handling food as people may carry food poisoning bacteria on their hands; coughing and sneezing near food can contaminate it; clothing, aprons must be clean; cuts must be covered with a bright coloured plaster to prevent infections passing to food; ensure nails are clean; remove jewellery; a food handler with diarrhoea, vomiting etc. should not prepare or cook food; etc.", marks: null, contextNote: "Explain this required heading. Original2018 source wording and temperatures retained." },
    { id: "heading-3", kind: "point", verbatim: "Reheating procedures: food must be heated to a temperature high enough to destroy bacteria, or it may cause food poisoning; a temperature of 100°C for at least five minutes is necessary to destroy bacteria; high risk foods such as stews and gravies require heating to 100°C; reheating should be done quickly and thoroughly; reheat foods once only; if reheating in a microwave set at highest power setting; etc.", marks: null, contextNote: "Explain this required heading. Original2018 source wording and temperatures retained." },
    ],
